    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe overall design is strong and idiomatic for this codebase: `FlagRiskFlag` follows the `Flag<X>` naming/YAML convention, correctly reuses the already-decided `possibleDuplicate`/`capacityWarning` flags rather than recomputing them, delegates the email rule to the shared `OwnerEmail` authority, and the `@Transient` field + `@Transient` getter pattern matches the existing `capacityWarning`/`possibleDuplicate` flags. That part needs no change.\n\nOne structural finding:\n\n- **Duplicated domain extraction in `OwnerEmail`.** `isDisposableAdjacent` (lines 61\u201381) re-implements the same `lastIndexOf('@')` \u2192 `substring(at + 1)` \u2192 lower-case domain extraction that `isDisposableDomain` (lines 42\u201351) already does. Worse, the two copies have quietly drifted: `isDisposableDomain` does `.toLowerCase()` with no trim/empty guard, while `isDisposableAdjacent` does `.toLowerCase().trim()` plus an `isEmpty()` check. That inconsistency is exactly the smell duplication produces. Extract a private `domainOf(String email)` helper (returning the lower-cased, trimmed domain or `null`/empty when absent) and have both `isDisposableDomain` and `isDisposableAdjacent` call it, so the two blocklist checks share one definition of \"the domain of this address.\" This keeps `OwnerEmail` the single source of truth it documents itself to be, rather than two subtly different parsers.\n\nMinor, optional: the disposable \"base label\" (`disposable.substring(0, disposable.indexOf('.'))`) is derived inside the per-call loop; if a maintainer later wants to treat adjacency as first-class it would read more naturally as a precomputed set alongside `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` \u2014 but that's a judgment call, not required.",
